const a={"the-old-hall-ely":[2e3,1125],"couple-dancing":[2048,1370],"guests-dancing":[2048,1365],"band-and-dance-floor":[2048,1365],"five-piece-band":[2048,1365],"dance-floor-moves":[1e3,648]},e=(t,n,o)=>({type:"image",src:`/images/blog/jen-and-tom/${t}.webp`,alt:n,caption:o,width:(a[t]||[1e3,667])[0],height:(a[t]||[1e3,667])[1]}),r={slug:"jen-and-toms-wedding",title:"Jen & Tom’s Wedding at The Old Hall Ely",metaTitle:"Jen & Tom’s Wedding at The Old Hall Ely | The Supreme Collective",metaDescription:"Revisit Jen and Tom’s April 2017 wedding at The Old Hall Ely in Cambridgeshire, with a five-piece party band and photographs from their celebration.",excerpt:"A five-piece party band, a star-lit marquee and a Cambridgeshire celebration: revisit Jen and Tom’s wedding at The Old Hall Ely.",category:"Real Weddings",author:"Rhona Downie",authorType:"Person",publishedAt:"2017-04-23",readTime:"3 min read",heroImage:"/images/blog/jen-and-tom/first-dance.webp",heroImageAlt:"The bride and groom sharing their first dance on a chequered floor, with the five-piece band onstage behind them",heroImageFit:"contain",heroImageWidth:2048,heroImageHeight:1365,content:[{type:"paragraph",text:"On 1 April 2017, we sent our five-piece party band—two vocalists, guitar, bass and drums—to The Old Hall Ely in Cambridgeshire. And firstly, may I say, don’t hold the words “old hall” against this venue. There was no musty old town hall in sight; instead, the band rolled up to this gorgeous, picturesque venue."},e("the-old-hall-ely","The Old Hall Ely’s brick manor house and landscaped gardens, with Ely Cathedral visible in the distance","The Old Hall Ely, Cambridgeshire."),{type:"paragraph",text:"The band members arrived at 5pm, giving them 90 minutes for setup and soundcheck. They began setting up in the marquee, which was permanently attached to the venue. The marquee already had a black star-lit effect on the walls and ceiling, and led straight to the dining area."},{type:"paragraph",text:"They worked quickly and quietly, since the wedding guests were just on the other side of the door, while waiting for the green light from Alex Moresby, wedding planner and stylist extraordinaire, to fit soundcheck around the speeches as requested by Jen."},{type:"paragraph",text:"After soundcheck, the band grabbed a meal from the BBQ to fuel up. They also posed for a photograph with our official Supreme Collective photographer, Natalja Safronova, whom we had brought along before the doors opened and the party began."},e("five-piece-band","Two vocalists, a guitarist, a bassist and a drummer posing onstage beneath coloured lights and a star-lit backdrop","The five-piece band before the party."),{type:"heading",text:"Photographs from the celebration"},e("guests-mingling","Wedding guests chatting at tables with white flowers beneath the marquee’s warm fairy lights"),e("wedding-guests","The groom and three wedding guests posing together beside the reception tables"),e("couple-dancing","The bride and groom dancing together among their guests beneath pink party lighting"),e("band-and-dance-floor","Wedding guests filling the chequered dance floor while the live band performs beneath a star-lit backdrop"),e("dance-floor-moves","Two wedding guests showing their dance moves as friends cheer around the chequered floor"),e("guests-with-band","The groom dancing in the middle of a circle of wedding guests beside the live band"),e("party-dancing","Two wedding guests dancing beneath the marquee’s star-lit ceiling"),e("guests-dancing","Wedding guests laughing and dancing together under colourful lights"),e("dancing-close-up","A wedding guest dancing in front of the band’s microphones and drum kit"),{type:"paragraph",text:"Wedding-day photography: Natalja Safronova. These photographs and the account above are from the 2017 celebration; venue layouts and arrangements may have changed since then."},{type:"heading",text:"Planning your own Cambridgeshire wedding?"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Thinking about the look of your evening party? Our wedding band lighting guide explains what to ask about stage lights, dance-floor effects and the lighting your venue already provides.",links:[{text:"wedding band lighting guide",href:"/blog/essential-stage-lighting-for-your-wedding-band/"}]},{type:"paragraph",text:"Explore our wedding bands in Cambridgeshire, browse the venue guide, or send us your date and venue to talk through the music for your celebration.",links:[{text:"wedding bands in Cambridgeshire",href:"/wedding-bands-cambridgeshire/"},{text:"venue guide",href:"/venues/?county=Cambridgeshire"},{text:"send us your date and venue",href:"/acts/"}]},{type:"paragraph",text:"For current venue information, visit The Old Hall Ely.",links:[{text:"The Old Hall Ely",href:"https://theoldhallely.co.uk/"}]}]},i={slug:"essential-stage-lighting-for-your-wedding-band",title:"Essential Stage Lighting for Your Wedding Band",metaTitle:"Wedding Band Lighting: What to Ask Before Booking | The Supreme Collective",metaDescription:"Plan your wedding band lighting: stage lights, dance-floor effects, venue uplighting and the questions to ask about equipment, space and smoke or haze.",excerpt:"What lighting do you need for your wedding band? A practical guide to stage lights, dance-floor effects and checking what your booking includes.",category:"Wedding Planning",author:"Rhona Downie",authorType:"Person",publishedAt:"2017-05-08",readTime:"5 min read",heroImage:"/images/blog/jen-and-tom/band-and-dance-floor.webp",heroImageAlt:"A live wedding band and guests on a chequered dance floor beneath colourful stage lights and a star-lit marquee ceiling",heroImageWidth:2048,heroImageHeight:1365,heroImageFit:"contain",content:[{type:"paragraph",text:"Great wedding music deserves a setting that feels ready for a party. Lighting helps bring the band and dance floor together, but you do not need to know the name of every fixture to make a good plan. Start with three questions: what is included with your chosen band, what does your venue already provide, and what atmosphere would you like?"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Originally published on 8 May 2017; refreshed on 22 October 2026. The equipment discussed here is a guide to the options, not a promise that every band supplies the same lighting. Confirm your chosen act’s current package and any extras before booking."},{type:"heading",text:"Stage lighting and dance-floor lighting do different jobs"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Stage lighting helps guests see the musicians, while dance-floor lighting adds colour and movement around the party. Decorative venue lighting, such as uplighting around the room or a star-lit ceiling, contributes another layer. Check these separately: an attractive room does not necessarily mean the band or dance floor will be lit."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Tell us where the musicians and dancing will be positioned, especially if they are in different spaces. A photograph of the room and an indication of the available performance area can help everyone agree an appropriate setup."},{type:"heading",text:"Disco T-bars and compact lighting bars"},{type:"paragraph",text:"A lighting bar combines several fixtures on a stand, making it an option for adding colour around a compact performance area. Depending on the equipment, lights may be aimed towards the band or dance floor and may use automatic, sound-activated or controlled programmes. A sound-activated effect responds to sound; it is not a guarantee that every flash will follow the beat in the same way."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Ask what your band’s lighting actually covers rather than relying on the phrase “lighting included”. Is it mainly for the performers, mainly for dancing, or both? Does it suit the size and layout of your room? If you prefer a gentle colour wash to lots of movement, mention that when discussing the setup."},{type:"heading",text:"LED party effects and disco balls"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Our original article also featured an LED disco-ball effect. This is worth distinguishing from a traditional mirrored disco ball: equipment described as a “disco ball” can produce quite different looks. Ask for a photograph or a recent performance clip showing the proposed effect, rather than assuming a particular fixture will be supplied."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Think about how you want the evening to feel. You might prefer a softer look for your first dance and more colour for the party afterwards. Discuss that with the band, including any effects you would rather avoid."},{type:"image",src:"/images/blog/jen-and-tom/first-dance.webp",width:2048,height:1365,alt:"The bride and groom sharing their first dance in front of a live band, with pink light across the chequered dance floor",caption:"Jen and Tom’s 2017 wedding at The Old Hall Ely. Wedding-day photography: Natalja Safronova. This historic photograph illustrates the setting, not a current equipment package."},{type:"heading",text:"What does the venue already provide?"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Before adding extras, ask your venue about its existing room and dance-floor lighting, who operates it, and whether the settings can change during the evening. Also check where stands can go, what space needs to remain clear, and whether the band needs to coordinate setup with the venue’s team."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Our venue guide is a useful starting point for planning the music, but confirm current arrangements directly with your venue. Layouts and equipment can change between weddings.",links:[{text:"venue guide",href:"/venues/"}]},{type:"heading",text:"Check before planning smoke, haze or flashing effects"},{type:"paragraph",text:"If you are considering smoke, fog or haze, get the venue’s approval before arranging it and ask the supplier to agree its use with the venue team. Do not assume it is allowed just because you have seen it in another wedding video. Ask about any alarm-system or room restrictions, and flag any concerns about flashing effects or guest sensitivities in advance so alternatives can be discussed."},{type:"heading",text:"Your wedding lighting checklist"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Before confirming the booking, agree: the lighting included with the chosen act; the stage and dance-floor areas it will cover; any venue lighting you will use; setup space and access; the look you want for the first dance and party; any restricted effects; and the price of any agreed extras. Keep those details with your booking arrangements so the band, venue and any DJ are working to the same plan."},{type:"heading",text:"See a real wedding, then plan your own"},{type:"paragraph",text:"For an example of a band performing in a star-lit marquee, explore Jen and Tom’s wedding at The Old Hall Ely. Their celebration shows how the band, room and dance floor can come together, although today’s arrangements should always be checked separately.",links:[{text:"Jen and Tom’s wedding at The Old Hall Ely",href:"/blog/jen-and-toms-wedding/"}]},{type:"paragraph",text:"Ready to find your band? Browse our wedding bands and add your date and venue, or get in touch to discuss the music and lighting you have in mind. We can help you check the details with your chosen act.",links:[{text:"Browse our wedding bands",href:"/acts/"},{text:"get in touch",href:"/contact/"}]}]},s=[{slug:"5-ways-live-music-transforms-an-event",title:"5 Ways Live Music Transforms an Event",metaTitle:"5 Ways Live Music Transforms an Event | Wedding Entertainment Ideas",metaDescription:"Discover 5 ways live music transforms weddings and events, from atmosphere and emotion to guest energy and unforgettable dance floor moments.",excerpt:"From atmosphere and emotion to guest energy and unforgettable moments, here are 5 ways live music can completely transform a wedding or event.",category:"Wedding Entertainment",author:"The Supreme Collective",publishedAt:"2026-03-11",readTime:"6 min read",heroImage:"/images/blog/live-music-transforms-event-hero.jpg",heroImageAlt:"Wedding guests celebrating together beneath red heart-shaped balloons",content:[{type:"paragraph",text:"When people think about booking wedding entertainment, they often picture one thing first: a packed dance floor."},{type:"paragraph",text:"And yes, that is a huge part of it."},{type:"paragraph",text:"There is nothing quite like seeing a room come alive, watching guests let go, and hearing afterwards that people danced so hard their feet were aching for days. We are always so grateful to receive messages like that after an event, because it reminds us just how powerful live music can be. Great music does not just fill a room with sound. It creates moments that stay with people for years."},{type:"paragraph",text:"But the truth is, live music does much more than get people dancing."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Whether you are planning a wedding, a private party or a corporate celebration, live music changes the entire feel of an occasion. It transforms the atmosphere, lifts guest energy, creates emotional highlights and makes the whole experience feel more immersive and memorable."},{type:"paragraph",text:"As we head into the busier half of the year, we have some incredible events coming up. Some clients are using the band to build the perfect atmosphere before handing over to a DJ later in the evening. Others are planning rustic, festival-style weddings with acoustic performances woven into the day. Later in the year, corporate summer parties and Christmas events will bring another kind of energy altogether."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Every one of them is a reminder that live music does not just entertain people. It moves them."},{type:"heading",text:"1. It sets the atmosphere from the very first moment"},{type:"paragraph",text:"One of the most powerful things about live music is how quickly it can shape the mood of an event."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Before the dance floor is even open, the music is already doing its job. A mellow acoustic set can make a drinks reception feel warm and welcoming. A soulful live performance can make dinner feel elegant and intimate. A carefully paced evening set can take guests from relaxed conversation to full celebration in a way that feels completely natural."},{type:"paragraph",text:"That is one of the biggest advantages of live music for wedding receptions and private events. Live musicians can adapt in real time. They are not just pressing play. They are reading the room, responding to the atmosphere and helping the event unfold with the right feeling at the right moment."},{type:"paragraph",text:"For some of our upcoming weddings, the band is being used as a beautifully judged build-up to the later party. The first set is mellow and stylish, the second set lifts into something more upbeat, and the final set gets everyone onto the dance floor before handing over to the DJ. It is a brilliant example of how a wedding band and DJ can work together to create a seamless evening."},{type:"heading",text:"2. It creates emotional moments people actually remember"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Live music has a way of making moments land more deeply."},{type:"paragraph",text:"It could be the emotion of hearing a favourite song performed live. It could be the warmth of an acoustic set during an outdoor drinks reception. It could be the goosebumps that come from an incredible vocalist delivering a song at exactly the right point in the evening."},{type:"paragraph",text:"There is something human, immediate and personal about live performance that recorded music cannot quite replicate. It feels more present. More real. More connected."},{type:"paragraph",text:"That is why live music works so beautifully across so many different parts of a celebration. It is not only about the evening party. It can add real value to the entire event, from ceremony music and drinks receptions to the final dance floor set."},{type:"paragraph",text:"For couples searching for live music for wedding reception ideas, this is often the real magic. Live music does not just sound good. It makes the important moments feel bigger."},{type:"image",src:"/images/blog/emotional-moment.jpg",alt:"Couple sharing an emotional wedding dance surrounded by guests and sparklers"},{type:"heading",text:"3. It turns guests from spectators into participants"},{type:"paragraph",text:"A great live band does not just perform at people. It draws them in."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Guests respond differently to live music because it feels interactive and alive. They sing along more. They dance sooner. They cheer louder. They connect more easily. Even guests who thought they would stay seated often find themselves getting up for one song and then staying on the dance floor far longer than they expected."},{type:"paragraph",text:"This is where live music can completely transform guest energy."},{type:"paragraph",text:"At some events, that interaction becomes part of the entertainment itself. Think roaming acoustic musicians performing among guests in a festival-style setting. A saxophonist stepping onto the dance floor. A vocalist using a wireless mic to join the crowd. Guests having an impromptu dance-off with the band."},{type:"paragraph",text:"These are the kinds of moments people remember because they feel spontaneous, exciting and genuinely unique."},{type:"image",src:"/images/blog/audience-interaction.jpg",alt:"Singer interacting with a bride during a live performance"},{type:"paragraph",text:"If you are looking for bands to play at weddings or private events, this is one of the biggest advantages of choosing a strong live act. The entertainment becomes part of the experience, not just background noise."},{type:"heading",text:"4. It gives the event more personality and style"},{type:"paragraph",text:"No two events are the same, and the entertainment should reflect that."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Some couples want elegant, polished live music throughout the day. Some want a high-energy luxury wedding band feel for the evening. Others want something more relaxed and unexpected, such as acoustic roaming sets during the afternoon. Some are looking for a funk and soul wedding band to bring warmth and groove. Others want a Motown wedding band vibe that gets every generation onto the dance floor."},{type:"paragraph",text:"That is the beauty of live music. It can be tailored to the personality of the event."},{type:"paragraph",text:"We have weddings coming up where the live band is there to create a sophisticated build before a DJ-led late-night finish. We have rustic, festival-style celebrations where the music moves with the guests and creates surprise moments throughout the day. Later in the year, we will also be performing at corporate summer parties and Christmas events where the music helps people relax, celebrate and connect outside of the usual work setting."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Whether you are looking for wedding musicians in London, a live band for a wedding in the UK, or a versatile party band for hire, live music brings personality in a way that makes the event feel far more bespoke."},{type:"image",src:"/images/blog/roaming-band.jpg",alt:"Roaming live musicians performing outdoors at an event"},{type:"heading",text:"5. It creates memories guests talk about long after the event"},{type:"paragraph",text:"The best events are not just the ones that look beautiful on the day. They are the ones people keep talking about afterwards."},{type:"paragraph",text:"And time and again, live music becomes one of the most talked-about parts of an event. Guests remember how it felt when the band kicked in. They remember the moment the dance floor filled. They remember the singer’s voice, the saxophone solo, the crowd interaction and the song that everyone shouted back word for word."},{type:"image",src:"/images/blog/sax-in-crowd.jpg",alt:"Saxophonist performing among guests on a packed dance floor"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Those are the moments that last."},{type:"paragraph",text:"For us, that is one of the greatest privileges of what we do. We love music, and we perform for people who love music. We get to help create joy, connection, release, celebration and nostalgia, sometimes all in the same night. We are there for the emotional journey of the event, and we want people to leave feeling on top of the world."},{type:"paragraph",text:"That is why booking live music is about much more than hiring a band."},{type:"paragraph",text:"It is about creating an atmosphere your guests can feel. It is about making the celebration more immersive, more energetic and more memorable. It is about turning a great event into an unforgettable one."},{type:"heading",text:"Final thoughts: why live music makes such a difference"},{type:"paragraph",text:"If you are considering entertainment hire for weddings, planning your evening party, or exploring ideas for live music for wedding receptions, a great live band is one of the most powerful ways to elevate the experience."},{type:"paragraph",text:"From atmosphere and emotion to energy and interaction, live music transforms the feel of an event in a way few other entertainment options can."},{type:"paragraph",text:"A brilliant live band does not just play songs. It creates moments. And those moments are often the ones your guests remember most."}]},i,r];export{s as b};
